WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Internal network abuse occurs when user-controlled input influences a server-side request.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.780
Features such as URL previews, webhook testing, file import,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.320
and document conversion may reach systems that attackers cannot access directly.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.820
Metadata service attacks are common in cloud environments.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.840
If a workload can reach metadata endpoints,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.380
attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or environmental details.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.480
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-supplied destinations as

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:35.100
safe. URL allowlists, protocol restrictions, DNS handling,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.880
and redirect controls must be designed carefully.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.220
SSRF defenses work best when application validation is paired with network segmentation,

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.080
egress filtering, boundary protection,

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.900
and monitoring for unusual server-initiated traffic.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.160
Use network segmentation and boundary protection.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.740
Restrict destinations with URL allowlists where possible.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.440
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual server-initiated requests.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:09.580
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include SC.L2-3.13.1 - Boundary protection;

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.600
AC.L2-3.1.3 - Information flow control.
